  <section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Buyer guide</p><h1>Website ownership and handover</h1><p class="lede">Agree who controls the accounts and what you can take with you before the build begins.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><article class="wrap prose"><nav class="guide-contents" aria-label="In this guide"><h2>In this guide</h2><ol><li><a href="#keep-business-accounts-identifiable">Keep business accounts identifiable</a></li><li><a href="#separate-bespoke-work-from-reusable-software">Separate bespoke work from reusable software</a></li><li><a href="#make-exports-useful">Make exports useful</a></li><li><a href="#describe-the-practical-handover">Describe the practical handover</a></li><li><a href="#put-the-agreement-alongside-the-scope">Put the agreement alongside the scope</a></li></ol></nav>
    <p>By Wear Valley Digital · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-02">2 October 2026</time></p>
    <h2 id="keep-business-accounts-identifiable" tabindex="-1">Keep business accounts identifiable</h2>
    <p>Record the domain registrar, hosting account and connected services. Agree who owns each account, who can administer it and how the business recovers access. Give developers the access they need through individual accounts where the service supports them.</p>
    <p>A domain name, the website files and the account that hosts them are separate things. Being able to edit a page does not establish control of all three.</p>
    <h2 id="separate-bespoke-work-from-reusable-software" tabindex="-1">Separate bespoke work from reusable software</h2>
    <p>Ask the quote to identify the work created specifically for your project and the existing components it uses. Confirm the rights to use, maintain and move each part, including any third-party licence conditions.</p>
    <p>Reusing a tested component can reduce repeated work. Its licence and any service dependencies should still be clear. Ask which costs continue after the build and who pays them.</p>
    <h2 id="make-exports-useful" tabindex="-1">Make exports useful</h2>
    <p>List the content, images, files and business data you need to export. Agree a readable format and what is needed to use it elsewhere. Check whether an export includes relationships, configuration and uploaded files as well as individual records.</p>
    <p>Account access and credentials need their own handover process. Keep secrets out of ordinary content exports and agree how access will be transferred or replaced.</p>
    <h2 id="describe-the-practical-handover" tabindex="-1">Describe the practical handover</h2>
    <p>Agree who provides the source files, configuration information and operating instructions; who changes domain settings; and who checks the site after a move. Identify work that needs a separate quote, along with any notice period or third-party constraints.</p>
    <p>A useful handover check is whether another authorised developer can understand how to run the agreed solution and recover its data using the information provided.</p>
    <h2 id="put-the-agreement-alongside-the-scope" tabindex="-1">Put the agreement alongside the scope</h2>
    <p>Record ownership, licences, access, exports and ongoing responsibilities in the project agreement. Resolve unclear dependencies before accepting the quote. These questions help you prepare that agreement; your agreed project terms set the actual rights and obligations.</p>
